{"item_id":"E-OPT-406","title":"Why testimonial consent is not the whole check","body":"If you ever want to use a customer story, testimonial consent is only one gate. It does not answer the harder question: does the wording still match what was actually delivered, and does it avoid implying capability you have not shown?

For a small business owner, that matters because a loose testimonial can drift into a promise. Someone may agree to be quoted, but that does not automatically mean they approved every channel, every use, or every implied result. It also does not turn a quote into proof. A line about a pilot is still a pilot. It is not evidence of scale, ROI, or a finished system unless those points are explicitly supported.

The practical next step is simple: before any story is published, write down three things in plain English — what was actually done, what the person agreed could be said, and what claims are off limits. Then check whether the wording still matches the real scope. If it reads like a guarantee, it needs another pass. That second pass is cheaper than cleaning up a muddled claim later, which is the sort of admin nobody misses. Human approval should stay in the loop before anything goes public."}

